BASE LAYOUT
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 1 — Core Engineering Principles
# Version: 1.0
# 1. Purpose
This document defines the core engineering principles for Banynova.
Its purpose is to establish a consistent foundation for designing, developing, maintaining, and scaling the entire platform while ensuring high standards of performance, accessibility, security, maintainability, and user experience.
Every future module—including the Homepage, Tool Pages, Category Pages, Blog, Search, Tutorials, and shared components—must follow these engineering standards.
---
# 2. Project Vision
Banynova aims to become a modern browser-based platform offering high-quality online tools that are:
• Fast
• Reliable
• Accessible
• Privacy-focused
• Mobile-friendly
• Free to use
Every engineering decision should reinforce these values.
---
# 3. Engineering Philosophy
The engineering philosophy of Banynova is built on six core principles.
## 3.1 Performance First
Performance is considered a feature rather than an optimization.
Every page should load quickly, remain responsive during interaction, and minimize unnecessary CPU, memory, and network usage.
Engineering decisions should always prioritize rendering efficiency and Core Web Vitals.
---
## 3.2 Privacy by Design
User privacy is a core product principle.
Whenever technically possible:
• Process data locally within the browser.
• Avoid unnecessary network requests.
• Do not collect personal information without a legitimate purpose.
• Minimize third-party dependencies.
Privacy should be considered during architecture, implementation, and future feature development.
---
## 3.3 Simplicity Over Complexity
Prefer simple, readable, and maintainable solutions over unnecessary abstraction.
Avoid introducing additional complexity unless it provides measurable long-term benefits.
Simple code is easier to understand, debug, maintain, and extend.
---
## 3.4 Component-First Architecture

All user interface elements should be designed as reusable components.
Avoid creating one-off layouts or page-specific implementations whenever a reusable component can be used instead.
Examples include:
• Buttons
• Cards
• Badges
• Forms
• Navigation
• Search Components
• Dialogs
• Alerts
• Footers

Consistency improves maintainability and scalability.
---
## 3.5 Progressive Enhancement
The website should remain functional even if advanced browser features are unavailable.
Core functionality must not depend entirely on JavaScript, animations, or optional APIs.
Enhancements should improve the experience without preventing users from accessing essential functionality.
---
## 3.6 Accessibility by Default
Accessibility is not an optional enhancement.
Every feature should be designed to support:
• Keyboard navigation
• Screen readers
• High contrast
• Reduced motion preferences
• Responsive layouts
• Semantic HTML

Accessibility requirements should be considered during design rather than added afterwards.
---
# 4. Technology Stack
The primary technology stack consists of:
• Blogger XML
• HTML5
• CSS3
• Vanilla JavaScript (ES6+)
Avoid introducing external frameworks unless a clear technical justification exists.
---
# 5. Framework Policy
The platform should remain lightweight.
Avoid using:
• Bootstrap
• Tailwind CSS
• jQuery
• React
• Vue
• Angular
Reusable internal components should replace framework dependencies whenever practical.
---
# 6. Browser Support
Target support for:
• Latest Chrome
• Latest Edge
• Latest Firefox
• Latest Safari
Older browsers should receive functional content through graceful degradation where feasible.
---
# 7. Design Principles
Every interface should follow these principles:
• Clear visual hierarchy
• Consistent spacing
• Readable typography
• Predictable interactions
• Responsive layouts
• Minimal visual clutter
Interfaces should emphasize clarity over decoration.
---
# 8. Performance Principles
Performance should be considered during every stage of development.
General guidelines:
• Minimize render-blocking resources.
• Reduce unnecessary DOM complexity.
• Prefer CSS over JavaScript where practical.
• Optimize images and SVGs.
• Reuse components instead of duplicating markup.
• Use lazy loading appropriately.
• Avoid expensive layout calculations.
Performance should be measured continuously rather than treated as a final optimization step.
# 9. Accessibility Principles
Target compliance with WCAG 2.2 Level AA.
General rules:
• Use semantic HTML.
• Preserve logical heading hierarchy.
• Provide descriptive alternative text.
• Ensure keyboard accessibility.
• Maintain visible focus indicators.
• Respect user motion preferences.
• Avoid relying solely on color to communicate information.
Accessibility should remain compatible with future components and layouts.
---
# 10. Security Principles
Security should be considered throughout development.
General guidelines:
• Validate all user input.
• Escape dynamic content appropriately.
• Avoid unnecessary inline scripts.
• Minimize third-party scripts.
• Prefer HTTPS resources.
• Respect Blogger platform security limitations.

Security improvements should never compromise usability.
# 11. SEO Principles
Every page should support search engine optimization by default.
Include:
• Meaningful page titles
• Meta descriptions
• Canonical URLs
• Open Graph metadata
• Twitter Card metadata
• Structured Data (JSON-LD)
SEO should be integrated into the development process rather than added after implementation.
---
# 12. Scalability Principles

The platform should scale without requiring architectural redesign.
Design for future support of:
• Hundreds of tools
• Multiple categories
• Tutorials
• Blog posts
• Search
• Documentation
• Shared UI components
Core architecture should remain stable as new modules are introduced.
---
# 13. Code Quality Standards
All code should be:
• Readable
• Consistent
• Reusable
• Maintainable
• Well documented

Avoid:
• Duplicate code
• Unused code
• Magic numbers
• Deep nesting
• Inconsistent naming

Code reviews should prioritize clarity over cleverness.
---
# 14. Maintainability Principles
Every implementation should be easy to modify in the future.
Before adding new code, consider:
• Can this be reused?
• Will this scale?
• Does it follow existing patterns?
• Is it easy to understand?
• Will future developers recognize its purpose?

Maintainability is a long-term investment.
---
# 15. Engineering Checklist
Before implementing any feature, verify:
✓ Performance impact has been considered.
✓ Accessibility requirements are satisfied.

✓ Responsive behavior has been tested.
✓ Reusable components are preferred.
✓ Naming conventions are followed.
✓ SEO requirements are implemented.
✓ Security considerations have been reviewed.
✓ Code remains readable and maintainable.
✓ Future scalability has been considered.

---
# 16. Overall Engineering Principle
Every engineering decision should contribute to a platform that is:
• Fast by Design
• Private by Default
• Free for Everyone
The foundation should follow a component-first architecture that supports incremental growth without requiring structural redesign.
Long-term maintainability, consistency, accessibility, performance, and scalability should always take precedence over short-term convenience.
# End of Part 1
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 2 — Blogger XML Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose
This document defines the XML development standards for all Blogger templates used by Banynova.
Its purpose is to ensure every template remains:
• Consistent
• Maintainable
• Scalable
• Accessible
• Performance-focused
All future XML templates must follow these standards.
---
# 2. Base Template Philosophy
The Base_Layout.xml is the permanent foundation of the Banynova platform.
It should remain stable while future modules integrate into it.
Examples:
• Homepage
• Tool Pages
• Category Pages
• Blog
• Search
• Tutorials
• Static Pages
• Legal Pages
Future templates should extend the foundation rather than replace or duplicate it.
---
# 3. XML Organization
Maintain a predictable structure throughout the document.
Recommended order:
1. XML Declaration
2. Blogger Namespace
3. Head
4. SEO Metadata
5. CSS Variables
6. Base Styles
7. Layout Styles
8. Components
9. Utility Classes
10. Body
11. Header
12. Navigation
13. Main Content
14. Footer
15. JavaScript Modules
16. Initialization Scripts
Do not mix unrelated sections.
---
# 4. Naming Convention
Use a consistent naming convention.
Recommended prefix:
bn-
Examples:
bn-header
bn-nav
bn-main
bn-footer
bn-card
bn-btn
bn-grid
bn-search
bn-modal

Avoid generic names such as:
container
item
wrapper
box
content

Names should describe purpose rather than appearance.
---
# 5. Template Sections
Separate major template sections with clear comments.
Example:
==================================================================
HEADER
==================================================================

==================================================================
NAVIGATION
==================================================================

==================================================================
MAIN CONTENT
==================================================================

==================================================================
FOOTER
==================================================================

Large templates should remain easy to navigate.
---
# 6. XML Formatting
Use consistent formatting.
Rules:

• Two-space indentation
• Consistent line spacing
• One attribute per line when elements become long
• Closing tags aligned correctly
• No unnecessary whitespace

Readable XML is easier to maintain.
---
# 7. Head Section
The <head> should contain only essential resources.

Recommended order:
• Charset
• Viewport
• Theme Color
• Color Scheme
• Canonical
• Title
• Meta Description
• Open Graph
• Twitter Cards
• Icons
• Structured Data
• CSS
• Critical JavaScript
Avoid unnecessary external resources.
---
# 8. Metadata Standards
Every page should include:
• Title
• Description
• Canonical URL
• Robots
• Open Graph
• Twitter Cards

Homepage should additionally include:
• Organization Schema
• WebSite Schema
• SearchAction
• WebPage Schema

Tool pages should include:
• SoftwareApplication
• FAQPage
• BreadcrumbList
Blog posts should include:
• Article

---
# 9. CSS Placement
Global CSS belongs inside the Base Layout.
Avoid placing CSS inside individual widgets whenever possible.
Organize CSS into:
• Design Tokens
• Reset
• Typography
• Layout
• Components
• Utilities
• Responsive Rules
Avoid duplicate declarations.
# 10. JavaScript Placement
Keep JavaScript modular.
Recommended modules:
• Theme
• Storage
• Utilities
• Accessibility
• Performance
• Search
• Navigation

Avoid large monolithic scripts.
Each module should have a single responsibility.
---# 11. Blogger Expressions
Use Blogger expressions consistently.

Prefer:
<data:view.title/>
instead of duplicated literal values.
Use Blogger variables whenever available.
Avoid repeating the same expression multiple times.
---
# 12. Conditional Rendering
Use Blogger conditions only when necessary.
Example:
Homepage
Post Page
Static Page
Search Page
Archive Page
Keep nested conditions shallow.
Deep conditional nesting reduces readability.
---
# 13. Includes
Extract reusable sections into includes whenever practical.
Examples:
Header
Footer
Navigation
Search
Sidebar
Pagination
This improves reuse and simplifies maintenance.
---
# 14. Widget Strategy
Widgets should have a single responsibility.
Examples:
Navigation Widget
Search Widget
Footer Widget
Announcement Widget
Avoid combining unrelated functionality into one widget.
# 15. Image Standards
Always include:
loading="lazy"
decoding="async"
for below-the-fold images.
Do not lazy-load:
• Hero Images
• LCP Images
• Above-the-fold visuals

Prefer responsive image sizes where supported.
---
# 16. SVG Standards
Inline SVG should be used for:
• Logo
• Icons
• UI Graphics

If the same SVG is reused frequently, consider defining it once with:
<symbol>
and referencing it using:
<use>
When the SVG appears only a few times, reuse is optional and primarily improves maintainability rather than performance.
---
# 17. Theme Initialization
Initialize the saved theme before CSS is parsed.
Apply the data-theme attribute as early as possible.
Goals:
• Prevent FOUC
• Prevent layout shifts
• Preserve user preference
---
# 18. Performance Rules
Avoid unnecessary work.
Recommended practices:
• Minimize DOM depth
• Reduce duplicate markup
• Reuse components
• Use throttled scroll handlers
• Use passive event listeners
• Defer non-critical JavaScript

Performance should be considered throughout development.
# 19. Accessibility Rules
Every template should support:
• Semantic HTML
• Keyboard navigation
• Screen readers
• Visible focus indicators
• Proper heading hierarchy
• Reduced motion preferences

Accessibility is a default requirement.
---
# 20. XML Comments
Use comments to explain architecture rather than obvious code.
Good:
"Initialize theme before rendering to avoid FOUC."
Avoid:
"Button starts here."
Comments should explain intent, not implementation.
---
# 21. Error Prevention
Avoid:
• Duplicate IDs
• Inline styles
• Inline event handlers
• Hardcoded URLs
• Hardcoded colors
• Magic values

Prefer reusable variables and configuration.
---
# 22. Future Compatibility
Design XML templates so future modules can integrate without modifying the foundation.
Examples:

Homepage
Tool Directory
Categories
Search
Documentation
Blog
Legal Pages
The Base Layout should remain backward-compatible as the platform grows.
---
# 23. Code Review Checklist
Before publishing any XML template, verify:
✓ XML validates correctly.
✓ No duplicate IDs exist.
✓ Semantic HTML is preserved.
✓ Accessibility requirements are met.
✓ Metadata is complete.
✓ Structured data is valid.
✓ CSS remains modular.
✓ JavaScript is modular.
✓ Performance best practices are followed.
✓ Naming conventions are consistent.
✓ The template integrates cleanly with the Base_Layout.xml architecture.
---
# 24. Overall XML Principle
Every Blogger XML template should prioritize:
• Clarity
• Consistency
• Reusability
• Accessibility
• Performance
• Maintainability

The Base_Layout.xml should remain the single source of truth for shared architecture, while individual templates extend it through reusable components rather than duplicating functionality.
# End of Part 2
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 3 — HTML Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================
# 1. Purpose
This document defines the HTML development standards for Banynova.
The goal is to ensure every page is:
• Semantic
• Accessible
• Maintainable
• Responsive
• SEO-friendly
• Performance-optimized

These standards apply to every page across the platform, including the Homepage, Tool Pages, Category Pages, Blog, Search, Documentation, and Static Pages.
# 2. HTML Philosophy
HTML defines the structure and meaning of content.
Never use HTML solely for visual presentation.
CSS controls appearance.
JavaScript controls behavior.
Maintain a clear separation of concerns.
---
# 3. HTML Version
Always use HTML5.
Start every page with:
<!DOCTYPE html>
Use:
<html lang="en">
Replace the language code when creating localized versions.
---
# 4. Semantic HTML
Always prefer semantic elements over generic containers.

Preferred elements:
<header>
<nav>
<main>
<section>
<article>
<aside>
<footer>
<form>
<figure>
<figcaption>
Avoid replacing semantic elements with unnecessary <div> tags.

Semantic HTML improves accessibility, SEO, and maintainability.
---
# 5. Document Structure
Recommended order:
<html>
<head>
<body>
<header>
<nav>
<main>
<section>
<footer>
<script>
Keep the hierarchy shallow and predictable.

---
# 6. Heading Hierarchy
Each page must contain exactly one:
<h1>
Use headings in logical order.
Correct:
<h1>
<h2>
<h3>
<h4>
Avoid:
<h1>
<h3>
<h2>
Never skip heading levels without a valid structural reason.
---
# 7. Main Landmark
Every page must contain one:
<main>
Rules:
• Only one main element per page.
• Exclude repeated content such as headers and footers.
• Use for the primary page content only.
---
# 8. Navigation
Wrap primary navigation inside:
<nav>
Provide an accessible label when multiple navigation regions exist.
Example:
aria-label="Primary Navigation"
Current page should use:
aria-current="page"
---
# 9. Links
Links should describe their destination.
Good:
Download PDF
Image Converter
About Banynova
Avoid:
Click Here
Read More
Learn More
Open
Avoid opening internal links in a new tab.
---
# 10. Buttons
Use:
<button>
for actions.
Use:
<a>
only for navigation.
Never style links to behave like buttons without proper semantics.
---
# 11. Images
Every meaningful image requires descriptive:
alt
Examples:
Good:
alt="PNG to JPG Converter Interface"
Bad:
alt="image"
Decorative images:
alt=""
---
# 12. SVG
Prefer inline SVG for:
• Logo
• Icons
• UI Graphics

SVG should include:
role="img"
when appropriate.
Decorative SVG should be hidden from assistive technologies.
---
# 13. Forms
Every form control must have:
<label>
Associate labels correctly using:
for
and
id
Never rely on placeholder text as a replacement for labels.
---
# 14. Inputs

Use the most appropriate input type.

Examples:

email

url

search

number

password

date

Proper input types improve usability and accessibility.
# 15. Tables
Use tables only for tabular data.
Never use tables for layout.
Include:
<thead>

<tbody>

<th>
Use scope attributes where appropriate.
---
# 16. Lists
Use:
<ul>
for unordered content.
Use:
<ol>

for ordered sequences.
Avoid manually numbering list items.
---
# 17. Accessibility Attributes
Use ARIA only when native HTML cannot express meaning.
Rule:
Native HTML first.
ARIA second.
No ARIA is better than incorrect ARIA.
---
# 18. Interactive Elements
Every interactive element must be:
• Keyboard accessible
• Focusable
• Clearly visible
• Operable without a mouse
Avoid JavaScript-only interactions that cannot be reached by keyboard.
# 19. Focus Management
Maintain a visible focus indicator.

Never remove focus styles unless replaced with an equally visible alternative.
Logical tab order must follow the visual layout.
---
# 20. Responsive HTML
Write HTML that adapts naturally.
Avoid unnecessary wrapper elements.
Prefer flexible structures over deeply nested containers.
HTML should support responsive CSS rather than requiring it.
---
# 21. Metadata
Every page should include:

• Title
• Description
• Canonical URL
• Open Graph
• Twitter Cards
• Structured Data

Metadata should accurately describe the page content.

---

# 22. Lazy Loading

Apply:

loading="lazy"

decoding="async"

only to below-the-fold images.

Never lazy-load:

• Hero images
• LCP images
• Above-the-fold visuals

---

# 23. Embedded Content

Minimize third-party embeds.

If embedding external content:

• Load lazily where possible.
• Preserve accessibility.
• Maintain responsive layouts.

Avoid unnecessary iframes.

---

# 24. Data Attributes

Use:

data-*

attributes for JavaScript hooks.

Avoid using CSS classes solely for JavaScript targeting.

Example:

data-theme

data-tool

data-category

---

# 25. IDs and Classes

IDs must be unique.

Classes should describe purpose rather than appearance.

Good:

bn-search-input

bn-tool-card

bn-primary-button

Avoid:

blue-box

left-column

big-text

---

# 26. Whitespace

Maintain readable markup.

Use consistent indentation.

Avoid excessive nesting.

Group related elements logically.

Readable HTML reduces maintenance costs.

---

# 27. Comments

Use comments to explain structure or architecture.

Good:

<!-- Primary Navigation -->

<!-- Tool Grid -->

Avoid comments that simply restate the code.

---

# 28. Performance Guidelines

Keep HTML lightweight.

Avoid:

• Duplicate markup
• Empty containers
• Excessive nesting
• Unused elements

Every element should have a clear purpose.

---

# 29. SEO Guidelines

Use semantic structure to improve search engine understanding.

Ensure:

• One H1
• Proper headings
• Descriptive links
• Meaningful alt text
• Structured data
• Canonical URLs

Avoid duplicate page titles and descriptions.

---

# 30. Maintainability

HTML should be easy to read and modify.

Before adding new markup, ask:

• Is this semantic?
• Is it accessible?
• Is it reusable?
• Can it be simplified?
• Does it improve clarity?

Choose simplicity over unnecessary complexity.

---

# 31. Validation

Before publishing, verify:

✓ HTML validates successfully.

✓ No duplicate IDs exist.

✓ Heading hierarchy is correct.

✓ All forms have labels.

✓ Images include appropriate alt text.

✓ Links are descriptive.

✓ Keyboard navigation works.

✓ Responsive layout is preserved.

✓ Accessibility has been reviewed.

✓ SEO metadata is complete.

---

# 32. Overall HTML Principle

HTML is the structural foundation of every Banynova page.
Every element should communicate meaning, support accessibility, improve SEO, and remain easy to maintain.
Build semantic, clean, and reusable markup that prioritizes clarity over complexity, enabling the platform to scale consistently as new features and modules are introduced.
# End of Part 3
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 4 — CSS Engineering Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the CSS engineering standards for Banynova.

The objective is to build a styling system that is:

• Scalable
• Maintainable
• Performance-first
• Responsive
• Accessible
• Consistent

CSS should function as a reusable design system rather than page-specific styling.

---

# 2. CSS Philosophy

CSS is responsible only for presentation.

It must never contain application logic.

JavaScript controls behavior.

HTML defines structure.

Maintain a strict separation of concerns.

---

# 3. Design System Philosophy

Banynova follows a Design Token architecture.

Visual values should never be hardcoded inside components.

Instead, components consume reusable design tokens.

Benefits:

• Consistency
• Easy theme updates
• Better maintainability
• Faster redesigns

---

# 4. Design Tokens

Store all reusable values as CSS Custom Properties.

Recommended categories:

## Colors

--bn-primary

--bn-secondary

--bn-accent

--bn-success

--bn-warning

--bn-danger

--bn-surface

--bn-background

--bn-text

--bn-border

---

## Typography

--bn-font-family

--bn-font-size-xs

--bn-font-size-sm

--bn-font-size-md

--bn-font-size-lg

--bn-font-size-xl

--bn-line-height

--bn-font-weight

---

## Spacing

--bn-space-1

--bn-space-2

--bn-space-3

--bn-space-4

--bn-space-5

--bn-space-6

Use spacing tokens instead of fixed pixel values.

---

## Border Radius

--bn-radius-sm

--bn-radius-md

--bn-radius-lg

--bn-radius-xl

--bn-radius-full

---

## Shadows

--bn-shadow-xs

--bn-shadow-sm

--bn-shadow-md

--bn-shadow-lg

--bn-shadow-xl

---

## Duration

--bn-transition-fast

--bn-transition-base

--bn-transition-slow

---

## Z-Index

--bn-z-dropdown

--bn-z-sticky

--bn-z-modal

--bn-z-tooltip

Avoid arbitrary z-index values.

---

# 5. Naming Convention

Use descriptive component names.

Examples:

.bn-header

.bn-nav

.bn-btn

.bn-card

.bn-search

.bn-footer

.bn-grid

.bn-container

Avoid names based on appearance.

Bad:

.blue-box

.left-column

.big-text

---

# 6. CSS Architecture

Recommended order:

1. Design Tokens

2. Reset

3. Base Elements

4. Typography

5. Layout

6. Components

7. Utilities

8. Animations

9. Responsive Rules

Keep architecture predictable.

---

# 7. CSS File Organization

Separate styles logically.

Examples:

Variables

Reset

Typography

Layout

Navigation

Hero

Cards

Buttons

Forms

Footer

Utilities

Animations

Responsive

Never mix unrelated styles.

---

# 8. Layout Standards

Use Flexbox and Grid.

Avoid float-based layouts.

Every layout should:

• Stretch naturally

• Avoid fixed heights

• Support dynamic content

• Adapt to different screen sizes

---

# 9. Grid System

Use CSS Grid for two-dimensional layouts.

Examples:

Tool Directory

Categories

Homepage Sections

Feature Cards

Avoid fixed column counts.

Preferred:

repeat(auto-fit, minmax())

or

repeat(auto-fill, minmax())

---

# 10. Container Standards

Use a reusable container class.

Example responsibilities:

• Max width

• Horizontal padding

• Responsive margins

Avoid page-specific container implementations.

---

# 11. Responsive Design Rules

Design mobile-first.

Recommended breakpoints:

Small

Medium

Large

Extra Large

Breakpoints should represent layout changes rather than specific devices.

Avoid device-specific CSS.

---

# 12. Spacing System

Never use random spacing.

Always use spacing tokens.

Avoid:

margin:17px;

Prefer:

var(--bn-space-*)

Maintain consistent vertical rhythm.

---

# 13. Typography Rules

Typography should scale consistently.

Avoid hardcoded font sizes.

Maintain:

Readable line-height

Predictable spacing

Consistent hierarchy

Limit font families.

---

# 14. Component Design

Every component should be:

Reusable

Independent

Predictable

Documented

Avoid deeply nested selectors.

Example:

Good

.bn-card

.bn-card-title

.bn-card-description

Avoid

.page .wrapper .content .card h3

---

# 15. Utility Classes

Utility classes should solve one problem.

Examples:

.hidden

.visually-hidden

.text-center

.flex

.grid

.mt-auto

Avoid creating hundreds of utility classes.

Components remain the primary styling approach.

---

# 16. Color Rules

Never hardcode colors inside components.

Always reference design tokens.

Example:

var(--bn-primary)

instead of

#3b82f6

This simplifies future redesigns.

---

# 17. Theme Support

Light and Dark themes should share the same components.

Only design tokens should change.

Avoid duplicate component styles.

Theme switching should not duplicate CSS.

---

# 18. Responsive Images

Images should:

Scale naturally

Maintain aspect ratio

Never overflow containers

Support lazy loading where appropriate

Avoid fixed image dimensions unless required.

---

# 19. Performance Rules

Prefer:

transform

opacity

background-color

Avoid animating:

width

height

top

left

margin

padding

Layout-triggering properties increase rendering cost.

---

# 20. CSS Containment

For large tool listings and searchable directories, consider:

content-visibility: auto;

contain-intrinsic-size: 600px;

Benefits:

• Faster rendering

• Reduced layout work

• Better scrolling performance

Apply selectively rather than globally.

---

# 21. Motion Rules

Motion should communicate:

State

Feedback

Hierarchy

Avoid decorative animations that do not improve usability.

Animations must never delay user interaction.

Motion should enhance clarity rather than decoration.

---

# 22. Motion Accessibility

Always respect:

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)

Reduce or disable:

• Continuous animations

• Floating effects

• Hover transitions

• Parallax

• Infinite loops

Ensure every feature remains fully usable without animation.

---

# 23. Transition Rules

Prefer:

transition:
background-color,
color,
transform,
opacity;

Avoid:

transition: all;

Only animate required properties.

---

# 24. Hover Effects

Hover should provide feedback without distracting users.

Good examples:

• Slight elevation

• Border highlight

• Background-color change

• Shadow adjustment

Avoid excessive movement.

---

# 25. Shadow System

Use predefined shadow tokens.

Do not invent new shadow values for each component.

Maintain consistency across the UI.

---

# 26. Border Radius

Border radius should use design tokens.

Avoid random values.

Maintain visual consistency throughout the platform.

---

# 27. CSS Comments

Use comments to separate architectural sections.

Example:

==========================================================================
COMPONENTS
==========================================================================

Avoid commenting every individual selector.

---

# 28. Maintainability

Before adding new CSS ask:

Can this reuse an existing component?

Can this use a design token?

Can this become a utility?

Does it introduce duplication?

Prefer extending existing systems over creating new ones.

---

# 29. Validation Checklist

Before publishing verify:

✓ No duplicate declarations.

✓ No unused selectors.

✓ No unnecessary specificity.

✓ Design tokens are used consistently.

✓ Responsive behavior is tested.

✓ Components remain reusable.

✓ Motion respects reduced-motion preferences.

✓ Performance best practices are followed.

---

# 30. Overall CSS Principle

CSS should function as a scalable design system rather than a collection of page-specific styles.

Every rule should improve consistency, maintainability, accessibility, responsiveness, and performance.

The design token system should remain the single source of truth, enabling future redesigns, additional themes, and platform growth with minimal code changes.
# End of Part 4
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 5 — JavaScript Engineering Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the JavaScript engineering standards for Banynova.

The goal is to ensure JavaScript remains:

• Modular
• Lightweight
• Predictable
• Maintainable
• Accessible
• Performance-first

JavaScript should enhance the user experience without becoming a dependency for core content.

---

# 2. JavaScript Philosophy

JavaScript controls behavior, not structure or presentation.

Responsibilities include:

• User interaction
• State management
• Theme switching
• Search
• Performance enhancements
• Progressive enhancement

Avoid generating large HTML structures with JavaScript whenever server-side or semantic HTML is sufficient.

---

# 3. Progressive Enhancement

The website should remain functional even when JavaScript is unavailable.

Core content must remain accessible.

JavaScript should enhance functionality rather than replace it.

Examples:

✓ Theme switching

✓ Search suggestions

✓ Lazy loading

✓ Animations

These should improve the experience without preventing access to essential content.

---

# 4. Module Architecture

Organize JavaScript into small, focused modules.

Recommended modules:

• Theme

• Storage

• Navigation

• Search

• Accessibility

• Performance

• Utilities

• Lazy Loader

• Dialog

• Analytics (future)

Each module should have a single responsibility.

Avoid monolithic scripts.

---

# 5. Global Namespace

Expose only one global namespace.

Example:

Banynova

All modules should live under this namespace.

Example:

Banynova.theme

Banynova.search

Banynova.storage

Banynova.utils

Avoid polluting the global scope.

---

# 6. Coding Style

Use modern ES6+ syntax.

Prefer:

const

let

Arrow Functions

Template Literals

Optional Chaining

Nullish Coalescing

Avoid:

var

Implicit globals

Legacy JavaScript patterns

---

# 7. Event Handling

Use event delegation whenever practical.

Attach listeners to stable parent elements rather than individual children.

Benefits:

• Better performance

• Reduced memory usage

• Easier maintenance

Always remove listeners when no longer needed.

---

# 8. Event Listener Guidelines

Prefer:

addEventListener()

Avoid:

onclick

onchange

Inline event handlers

Always use passive listeners for scrolling and touch interactions when appropriate.

Example:

{ passive: true }

---

# 9. Performance Principles

JavaScript should minimize:

• Layout recalculations

• Forced synchronous reflows

• Unnecessary DOM updates

• Repeated queries

Prefer caching DOM references.

Batch updates whenever possible.

---

# 10. DOM Manipulation

Query the DOM once whenever practical.

Avoid repeated:

document.querySelector()

inside loops.

Cache frequently used elements.

Minimize DOM mutations.

---

# 11. Debounce

Use debounce for user input events.

Examples:

• Search input

• Window resize

• Live validation

Recommended delay:

200–300 ms

Debounce prevents unnecessary repeated execution.

---

# 12. Throttle

Use throttle for continuous events.

Examples:

• Scroll

• Mouse move

• Resize (when continuous updates are needed)

Recommended interval:

100 ms

Never attach unthrottled scroll handlers.

---

# 13. requestAnimationFrame

Use requestAnimationFrame()

for visual updates.

Examples:

• Animations

• Scroll effects

• Progress indicators

Avoid using timers for rendering work.

---

# 14. Lazy Loading

Use native browser capabilities whenever possible.

Preferred:

loading="lazy"

IntersectionObserver

Avoid scroll-based lazy loading.

Never lazy-load:

• Hero images

• LCP images

• Above-the-fold content

---

# 15. Theme Management

Theme switching should:

• Be instantaneous

• Persist across sessions

• Avoid layout shifts

• Prevent FOUC

Store only the selected theme.

Avoid duplicating component styles.

Prefer CSS variables over JavaScript styling.

---

# 16. Storage

Use localStorage only for lightweight preferences.

Examples:

• Theme

• User settings

• UI preferences

Do not store:

• Sensitive information

• Large datasets

• Temporary DOM state

Always validate stored values before use.

---

# 17. Search Architecture

Search should be modular and reusable.

Recommended flow:

Input

↓

Normalize Query

↓

Search Index

↓

Rank Results

↓

Render Results

↓

Keyboard Navigation

↓

Selection

Search logic should remain independent of UI rendering.

---

# 18. Search Performance

Avoid searching the DOM directly.

Instead:

• Build an in-memory index

• Normalize strings once

• Cache searchable data

Use debounce to reduce unnecessary searches.

---

# 19. Accessibility

Every JavaScript feature should support:

• Keyboard navigation

• Screen readers

• Visible focus

• Reduced motion

• ARIA updates where appropriate

JavaScript should never reduce accessibility.

---

# 20. Error Handling

Handle unexpected situations gracefully.

Avoid uncaught exceptions.

Log useful debugging information during development.

Fail gracefully in production.

---

# 21. Async Operations

Use:

async / await

instead of nested callbacks.

Always handle:

try

catch

finally

Avoid callback pyramids.

---

# 22. Network Requests

Minimize network requests.

Cache reusable data.

Avoid duplicate requests.

Cancel obsolete requests when appropriate.

Future APIs should support graceful failure.

---

# 23. Security

Never trust client-side input.

Always sanitize:

• Search queries

• URL parameters

• Dynamic HTML

Avoid:

eval()

new Function()

Unsafe HTML injection

Prefer:

textContent

instead of

innerHTML

unless sanitization is guaranteed.

---

# 24. Motion Performance

JavaScript should never animate layout-triggering properties.

Prefer:

transform

opacity

Let CSS handle animations whenever possible.

JavaScript should trigger state changes—not frame-by-frame animations.

---

# 25. Memory Management

Prevent memory leaks.

Remove:

• Event listeners

• Timers

• Observers

• References to removed elements

Unused objects should become eligible for garbage collection.

---

# 26. Utilities

Shared logic belongs inside:

Banynova.utils

Examples:

• debounce()

• throttle()

• clamp()

• escapeHTML()

• uniqueId()

Avoid duplicating helper functions across modules.

---

# 27. Documentation

Every public module should include:

Purpose

Parameters

Return Value

Dependencies

Side Effects

Document complex algorithms with concise comments.

---

# 28. Code Review Checklist

Before publishing JavaScript, verify:

✓ Modules have a single responsibility.

✓ No global variables are introduced.

✓ DOM queries are optimized.

✓ Scroll events are throttled.

✓ Input events are debounced.

✓ Passive listeners are used where appropriate.

✓ Accessibility remains intact.

✓ Theme initialization prevents FOUC.

✓ Search performance has been reviewed.

✓ No unused code remains.

---

# 29. Future Scalability

Design JavaScript modules so they can support:

• Hundreds of tools

• Multiple search indexes

• Additional themes

• Offline capabilities

• PWA features

• Plugin architecture (future)

Core modules should remain backward-compatible as new functionality is added.

---

# 30. Overall JavaScript Principle

JavaScript should enhance—not dominate—the user experience.

Every module should be modular, reusable, performant, accessible, and easy to maintain.

Favor simplicity over cleverness, native browser capabilities over heavy abstractions, and progressive enhancement over dependency-driven design.
The Banynova JavaScript architecture should remain lightweight, scalable, and resilient as the platform evolves.
# End of Part 5
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 6 — UI Components Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the UI Component standards for Banynova.

Every component should be:

• Reusable
• Accessible
• Responsive
• Performance-first
• Theme-aware
• Maintainable

Components should behave consistently across the Homepage, Tool Pages, Category Pages, Blog, Search, Tutorials, and future modules.

---

# 2. Component Philosophy

Every component should have a single responsibility.

A component should:

• Solve one UI problem
• Be reusable
• Be independent
• Be configurable
• Avoid page-specific logic

Never duplicate components for different pages.

---

# 3. Component Structure

Each component should follow:

Component

↓

Variants

↓

States

↓

Accessibility

↓

Responsive Behavior

↓

Theme Support

↓

Motion

Maintain a predictable structure.

---

# 4. Header

The Header is the primary navigation container.

It should contain:

• Logo
• Primary Navigation
• Search
• Theme Toggle
• Mobile Menu

Requirements:

• Sticky (optional)
• Lightweight
• Responsive
• Keyboard accessible

Avoid large promotional banners inside the header.

---

# 5. Navigation

Navigation should remain simple.

Recommended structure:

• Tools

• Categories

• Blog

• Resources

• About

Requirements:

✓ Semantic <nav>

✓ aria-label

✓ aria-current

✓ Keyboard support

✓ Mobile navigation

Avoid deep navigation hierarchies.

---

# 6. Hero Section

The Hero introduces the platform.

Recommended content:

• Main heading

• Short description

• Primary CTA

• Secondary CTA

• Search

• Illustration

Keep messaging concise.

Hero animations should remain subtle.

Never autoplay distracting effects.

---

# 7. Search Component

Search should be available across the platform.

Capabilities:

• Instant search

• Keyboard navigation

• Highlight matches

• Empty state

• Accessible suggestions

Use:

role="combobox"

where appropriate.

Search should remain functional without animations.

---

# 8. Tool Cards

Tool cards should provide quick access.

Recommended content:

• Icon

• Tool Name

• Description

• Category

Optional:

• Popular badge

• New badge

• Updated badge

Cards should maintain equal heights where practical.

---

# 9. Buttons

Buttons communicate actions.

Primary

Secondary

Outline

Ghost

Danger

Loading

Disabled

Buttons should:

• Have consistent padding

• Visible hover

• Visible focus

• Clear active state

Avoid oversized buttons.

---

# 10. Button Guidelines

Buttons should use verbs.

Examples:

Convert

Generate

Compress

Download

Analyze

Avoid vague labels like:

Submit

Go

Click

---

# 11. Badges

Badges indicate status.

Recommended badges:

New

Updated

Popular

Beta

AI

Free

Limited

Badges should never replace important content.

Use concise text.

---

# 12. Forms

Forms should remain simple.

Each input requires:

• Label

• Help text (when needed)

• Validation

• Error message

Avoid unnecessary fields.

---

# 13. Form Validation

Validation should occur:

• During submission

• Progressively where appropriate

Errors should explain:

• What happened

• How to fix it

Never rely only on color.

---

# 14. Input Fields

Recommended types:

search

email

url

number

password

date

textarea

Use appropriate autocomplete values.

---

# 15. Dropdowns

Dropdowns should:

• Open via keyboard

• Close with Escape

• Support arrow navigation

• Preserve focus

Avoid oversized dropdown menus.

---

# 16. Dialogs

Dialogs should:

Trap keyboard focus

Close with Escape

Restore focus after closing

Prevent background interaction

Dialogs should use:

role="dialog"

and

aria-modal="true"

---

# 17. Alerts

Alert types:

Success

Information

Warning

Error

Each alert should include:

• Icon

• Message

• Appropriate color

Critical alerts should be announced to assistive technologies.

---

# 18. Empty States

Every empty state should explain:

Why nothing is shown

What users can do next

Examples:

No Search Results

No Saved Tools

No Categories

No Articles

Include a helpful call-to-action.

---

# 19. Loading States

Use lightweight loading indicators.

Prefer:

Skeleton Screens

instead of spinning loaders for content.

Loading indicators should not block interaction unnecessarily.

---

# 20. Footer

Recommended links:

Contact

Privacy Policy

Terms

Cookies

Accessibility

Sitemap

Copyright

Footer should remain simple.

Avoid repeating navigation excessively.

---

# 21. Icons

Icons should support labels.

Avoid icon-only controls unless:

aria-label

is provided.

Icons should remain visually consistent.

---

# 22. Theme Support

Every component must support:

Light Theme

Dark Theme

High Contrast (future)

Only design tokens should change.

Never duplicate component CSS.

---

# 23. Responsive Behavior

Components should adapt naturally.

Examples:

Cards stack vertically.

Navigation collapses.

Buttons wrap.

Forms resize.

Avoid horizontal scrolling.

---

# 24. Motion

Motion should:

Communicate feedback

Support hierarchy

Improve usability

Avoid decorative animation.

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

---

# 25. Accessibility

Every component should support:

Keyboard Navigation

Screen Readers

Visible Focus

Color Contrast

Reduced Motion

Semantic HTML

Accessibility is mandatory.

---

# 26. Performance

Components should:

Avoid unnecessary DOM depth

Avoid duplicate markup

Reuse existing components

Minimize JavaScript

Prefer CSS solutions

Use lazy loading where appropriate

Performance should remain a primary consideration.

---

# 27. Naming Convention

Recommended:

.bn-btn

.bn-card

.bn-header

.bn-nav

.bn-footer

.bn-dialog

.bn-alert

.bn-search

.bn-form

Use consistent naming throughout the project.

---

# 28. Component Lifecycle

Each component should define:

Purpose

Variants

States

Accessibility

Responsive Behavior

Theme Support

Dependencies

Avoid undocumented behaviors.

---

# 29. Code Review Checklist

Before releasing any component:

✓ Accessible

✓ Responsive

✓ Theme-aware

✓ Reusable

✓ Keyboard-friendly

✓ Screen-reader compatible

✓ Performance reviewed

✓ Motion reviewed

✓ No duplicated logic

✓ Naming conventions followed

---

# 30. Overall UI Principle

Every UI component should provide a consistent, intuitive, and accessible user experience.

Components should be reusable building blocks rather than page-specific implementations.

Design for clarity over decoration, functionality over complexity, and consistency over customization.

The Banynova UI component library should remain scalable, maintainable, and future-ready, allowing new features and modules to integrate seamlessly without redesigning the core interface.

# End of Part 6
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 7 — Accessibility Standards (WCAG 2.2 AA)
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the accessibility standards for Banynova.

Accessibility is a core engineering requirement—not an optional enhancement.

Every page, component, and interaction must be designed to ensure equal access for all users, including people using assistive technologies.

Target compliance:

• WCAG 2.2 Level AA

These standards apply across the entire platform.

---

# 2. Accessibility Philosophy

Accessibility should be built into every stage of development.

Never treat accessibility as a post-development task.

Accessibility must influence:

• Design
• HTML Structure
• CSS
• JavaScript
• UI Components
• Content
• Documentation

Every feature should remain usable regardless of ability, device, or input method.

---

# 3. WCAG 2.2 AA Compliance

Banynova targets WCAG 2.2 Level AA.

Core principles:

• Perceivable
• Operable
• Understandable
• Robust

Every new feature should be reviewed against these four principles before release.

---

# 4. Semantic HTML

Always use semantic HTML first.

Preferred elements:

<header>

<nav>

<main>

<section>

<article>

<aside>

<footer>

<button>

<form>

<label>

Semantic HTML improves accessibility without additional ARIA.

---

# 5. Keyboard Navigation

Every interactive element must be fully operable using only the keyboard.

Users should be able to:

✓ Navigate using Tab

✓ Move backwards using Shift + Tab

✓ Activate controls using Enter

✓ Toggle buttons using Space

✓ Close dialogs using Escape

Mouse interaction must never be required.

---

# 6. Focus Management

Keyboard focus should always remain visible.

Requirements:

• Never remove the default focus indicator unless replacing it with a more visible alternative.
• Focus order must follow the visual layout.
• Focus should never become trapped except inside modal dialogs.
• Restore focus after closing dialogs or menus.

---

# 7. Focus Indicators

Provide clear focus styles.

Recommended:

• Outline
• Border
• Box Shadow

Focus indicators must have sufficient contrast against both light and dark themes.

Avoid relying only on color changes.

---

# 8. Skip Navigation

Every page should include a Skip to Content link.

Purpose:

• Bypass repeated navigation
• Improve keyboard navigation
• Improve screen reader usability

The link should become visible when focused.

---

# 9. Screen Reader Support

Ensure content is understandable by screen readers.

Guidelines:

• Use meaningful headings.
• Use proper landmark elements.
• Provide descriptive labels.
• Maintain logical reading order.
• Avoid redundant announcements.

Screen readers should be able to navigate the page efficiently.

---

# 10. Headings

Maintain a logical heading hierarchy.

Rules:

• One H1 per page
• No skipped heading levels
• Headings describe section content

Avoid using headings purely for styling.

---

# 11. Forms

Every form control requires:

• Associated label
• Instructions when needed
• Error message
• Validation feedback

Never rely solely on placeholders.

Required fields should be identified programmatically.

---

# 12. Form Validation

Validation messages should:

• Clearly describe the problem
• Explain how to fix it
• Be announced to assistive technologies
• Not rely solely on color

Users should not lose entered data after validation errors.

---

# 13. Images

Meaningful images require descriptive:

alt

Decorative images:

alt=""

Complex graphics should include additional descriptions when necessary.

---

# 14. Icons

Icons should not communicate information by themselves.

Provide:

• Visible labels

or

• aria-label

Decorative icons should be hidden from assistive technologies.

---

# 15. Links

Links should describe their destination.

Good:

Download PDF Guide

PNG to JPG Converter

Accessibility Documentation

Avoid:

Click Here

Read More

Learn More

---

# 16. Buttons

Buttons should clearly describe their action.

Examples:

Convert File

Generate Password

Copy Result

Avoid generic labels such as:

Submit

OK

Go

---

# 17. Color Contrast

Text should maintain sufficient contrast.

Target WCAG AA minimums:

• Normal text: 4.5:1
• Large text: 3:1

Avoid low-contrast color combinations.

Test both Light and Dark themes.

---

# 18. Color Usage

Never rely only on color to communicate meaning.

Always pair color with:

• Icons
• Labels
• Text
• Patterns where appropriate

Users with color vision deficiencies should receive the same information.

---

# 19. Reduced Motion

Respect user motion preferences.

Implement:

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)

Reduce or disable:

• Transitions
• Hover animations
• Floating effects
• Parallax
• Continuous loops
• Auto-playing decorative motion

All essential functionality must remain available without animation.

---

# 20. Motion Guidelines

Animations should:

• Improve feedback
• Clarify hierarchy
• Support usability

Animations must never:

• Delay interaction
• Hide information
• Cause distraction
• Trigger unnecessary motion

Motion should enhance clarity rather than decoration.

---

# 21. ARIA Guidelines

Use ARIA only when native HTML cannot express the required semantics.

Principles:

• Native HTML first
• ARIA second
• No ARIA is better than incorrect ARIA

Avoid unnecessary ARIA attributes.

---

# 22. Landmark Regions

Use landmarks consistently.

Recommended:

<header>

<nav>

<main>

<aside>

<footer>

Provide accessible names when multiple landmarks of the same type exist.

---

# 23. Live Regions

Use ARIA live regions for dynamic content.

Examples:

• Search results
• Status messages
• Form validation
• Notifications

Avoid excessive announcements.

Only announce meaningful changes.

---

# 24. Dialog Accessibility

Dialogs must:

• Use role="dialog"
• Include aria-modal="true"
• Trap keyboard focus
• Restore focus after closing
• Close with Escape

Background content should not remain interactive.

---

# 25. Responsive Accessibility

Accessibility should remain consistent across:

• Desktop
• Tablet
• Mobile

Touch targets should be comfortably sized.

Avoid interactions that depend on hover alone.

---

# 26. Content Readability

Content should be easy to read.

Guidelines:

• Short paragraphs
• Clear headings
• Consistent spacing
• Plain language
• Predictable terminology

Readable content benefits all users.

---

# 27. Testing

Accessibility testing should include:

✓ Keyboard-only navigation

✓ Screen reader testing

✓ Color contrast verification

✓ Reduced motion testing

✓ Responsive testing

✓ Focus order verification

✓ Form validation review

✓ Landmark inspection

Accessibility should be tested before every production release.

---

# 28. Accessibility Checklist

Before publishing verify:

✓ Semantic HTML is used.

✓ Heading hierarchy is correct.

✓ Images include appropriate alt text.

✓ Forms include labels.

✓ Keyboard navigation works.

✓ Focus indicators are visible.

✓ Color contrast meets WCAG AA.

✓ Reduced motion is supported.

✓ ARIA usage is valid.

✓ Screen reader experience has been reviewed.

---

# 29. Future Accessibility

Future features should continue supporting:

• Voice navigation

• High Contrast themes

• Additional language support

• Improved assistive technology compatibility

Accessibility improvements should remain backward-compatible.

---

# 30. Overall Accessibility Principle

Accessibility is a fundamental quality attribute of Banynova.

Every user—regardless of ability, device, or assistive technology—should be able to perceive, understand, navigate, and interact with the platform independently.

Engineering decisions should prioritize inclusive design, ensuring that accessibility, usability, and performance evolve together as the platform grows.

# End of Part 7
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 8 — Performance Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the performance engineering standards for Banynova.

Performance is treated as a core product feature—not a post-launch optimization.

Every page should be:

• Fast
• Responsive
• Efficient
• Stable
• Scalable

Performance should be considered during architecture, implementation, testing, and future maintenance.

---

# 2. Performance Philosophy

Every engineering decision should minimize:

• Network requests
• JavaScript execution
• Rendering work
• Layout recalculations
• Memory usage

Optimize before adding complexity.

The fastest code is the code that never executes.

---

# 3. Performance Goals

Target excellent real-world user experience.

Recommended targets:

✓ Lighthouse Performance ≥ 95

✓ Core Web Vitals: Good

✓ First Contentful Paint (FCP) ≤ 1.8 s

✓ Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) ≤ 2.5 s

✓ Interaction to Next Paint (INP) ≤ 200 ms

✓ Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) ≤ 0.1

Performance should be measured on mobile-first conditions.

---

# 4. Core Web Vitals

Core Web Vitals are primary performance indicators.

Monitor:

• LCP
• CLS
• INP

Optimize continuously rather than only before release.

---

# 5. Largest Contentful Paint (LCP)

The largest visible element should load quickly.

Common LCP elements:

• Hero heading
• Hero image
• Hero illustration
• Featured tool card

Guidelines:

✓ Avoid render-blocking resources.

✓ Preload critical assets when appropriate.

✓ Do not lazy-load the LCP image.

✓ Minimize server response time.

✓ Reduce CSS and JavaScript blocking.

---

# 6. Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)

Visual layout should remain stable.

Avoid:

• Images without dimensions

• Dynamic content pushing existing content

• Font swaps causing layout movement

• Delayed advertisements

Always reserve space for:

• Images

• Videos

• Embedded content

• Dynamic components

Target:

CLS ≤ 0.1

---

# 7. Interaction to Next Paint (INP)

User interactions should feel immediate.

Optimize:

• Clicks

• Keyboard input

• Search

• Menus

• Dialogs

• Forms

Avoid long JavaScript tasks.

Break expensive work into smaller operations.

Target:

INP ≤ 200 ms

---

# 8. JavaScript Performance

Keep JavaScript lightweight.

Guidelines:

• Minimize execution time.

• Remove unused code.

• Avoid synchronous heavy operations.

• Use native browser APIs.

• Defer non-critical scripts.

Prefer event delegation.

---

# 9. JavaScript Budget

Maintain a performance budget.

Recommended:

Initial JavaScript:

≤ 150 KB (compressed)

Critical JavaScript:

≤ 50 KB

Avoid unnecessary third-party libraries.

Every additional script should provide measurable value.

---

# 10. CSS Performance

Organize CSS efficiently.

Avoid:

• Duplicate rules

• Deep selectors

• Excessive specificity

Use:

• Design Tokens

• Reusable Components

• Utility Classes where appropriate

---

# 11. Critical CSS

Inline only the CSS required for above-the-fold rendering.

Non-critical styles should load after the initial render.

Goals:

• Faster First Paint

• Faster LCP

• Reduced render blocking

Avoid inlining the entire stylesheet.

---

# 12. Image Optimization

Images should be optimized before deployment.

Recommendations:

• Use modern formats (WebP, AVIF where supported)

• Compress images

• Resize appropriately

• Avoid oversized assets

• Use responsive images

Images should never be larger than required.

---

# 13. Responsive Images

Use responsive image techniques.

Prefer:

srcset

sizes

Serve images based on viewport size.

Avoid delivering desktop-sized images to mobile devices.

---

# 14. Lazy Loading

Apply lazy loading only to below-the-fold content.

Use:

loading="lazy"

decoding="async"

Do NOT lazy-load:

• Hero images

• Above-the-fold images

• LCP elements

Lazy loading should improve—not delay—the user experience.

---

# 15. SVG Optimization

Prefer SVG for:

• Logos

• Icons

• Simple illustrations

Optimize SVG files by removing unnecessary metadata.

Reuse symbols where practical.

---

# 16. Font Loading

Limit the number of font families and font weights.

Recommendations:

• Prefer system fonts where possible.

• Self-host fonts when practical.

• Preload critical fonts only.

• Use font-display: swap.

Avoid loading unused font variants.

---

# 17. Network Requests

Reduce total network requests.

Combine resources when appropriate.

Avoid duplicate downloads.

Cache reusable assets effectively.

Every request should have a clear purpose.

---

# 18. DOM Performance

Keep the DOM lightweight.

Avoid:

• Deep nesting

• Duplicate elements

• Empty wrappers

Large DOM trees increase rendering cost.

---

# 19. Rendering Performance

Prefer GPU-friendly properties.

Animate:

• transform

• opacity

• background-color

Avoid animating:

• width

• height

• top

• left

• margin

• padding

---

# 20. CSS Containment

For large directories and grids, consider:

content-visibility: auto;

contain-intrinsic-size: 600px;

Benefits:

• Faster rendering

• Reduced layout work

• Better scrolling performance

Apply selectively.

---

# 21. Scroll Performance

Never attach expensive scroll handlers.

Use:

• Throttle

• Passive listeners

• requestAnimationFrame() where appropriate

Avoid repeated layout calculations during scrolling.

---

# 22. Search Performance

Search should remain responsive.

Recommendations:

• Debounce input

• Cache searchable data

• Normalize once

• Avoid repeated DOM traversal

Search should scale efficiently as the number of tools grows.

---

# 23. Memory Management

Avoid memory leaks.

Remove:

• Event listeners

• Timers

• Observers

Release references to removed DOM nodes.

Monitor memory usage during long browsing sessions.

---

# 24. Third-Party Resources

Minimize third-party dependencies.

Before adding a new dependency, evaluate:

• Performance impact

• Privacy implications

• Maintenance cost

Remove unused third-party scripts promptly.

---

# 25. Progressive Enhancement

Performance optimizations should never break core functionality.

Users on older devices or slower networks should still be able to:

✓ Navigate

✓ Search

✓ Read content

✓ Use tools

Enhancements should improve—not replace—the base experience.

---

# 26. Performance Monitoring

Performance should be monitored continuously.

Recommended checks:

✓ Lighthouse

✓ Core Web Vitals

✓ Mobile Performance

✓ Bundle Size

✓ DOM Size

✓ Network Requests

✓ Accessibility Impact

---

# 27. Code Review Checklist

Before publishing:

✓ LCP optimized

✓ CLS minimized

✓ INP reviewed

✓ Images optimized

✓ Fonts optimized

✓ Critical CSS extracted

✓ JavaScript budget respected

✓ Lazy loading implemented correctly

✓ No unnecessary third-party scripts

✓ Performance tested on mobile

---

# 28. Future Scalability

Performance architecture should support:

• Hundreds of tools

• Large search indexes

• Category directories

• Blog growth

• Progressive Web App migration

Future modules should integrate without degrading existing performance.

---

# 29. Performance Budget

Recommended budgets:

HTML ≤ 50 KB
Critical CSS ≤ 20 KB

Initial JavaScript ≤ 150 KB

Critical JavaScript ≤ 50 KB

Largest Image ≤ 200 KB

Initial Requests ≤ 30

These budgets should be reviewed periodically as the platform evolves.
---
# 30. Overall Performance Principle
Performance is a continuous engineering discipline.
Every optimization should improve real user experience while preserving maintainability, accessibility, and scalability.
Banynova should remain lightweight, responsive, and efficient as the platform grows, ensuring that new features never compromise the speed and reliability of the existing foundation.
# End of Part 8
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 9 — SEO Engineering Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the Search Engine Optimization (SEO) standards for Banynova.

The objective is to ensure every page is:

• Discoverable

• Crawlable

• Indexable

• Shareable

• Structured

• Future-proof

SEO should be integrated into the engineering process from the beginning rather than added after development.

---

# 2. SEO Philosophy

SEO is a combination of:

• High-quality content

• Semantic HTML

• Technical optimization

• Performance

• Accessibility

• Structured data

Engineering decisions should support long-term organic visibility rather than short-term ranking tactics.

---

# 3. Page Metadata

Every indexable page must include:

• Unique Title

• Meta Description

• Canonical URL

• Viewport

• Charset

• Theme Color

• Robots Directive

Metadata should accurately represent the page's content.

---

# 4. Title Standards

Each page requires a unique title.

Recommended length:

50–60 characters

Structure:

Primary Topic | Banynova

Examples:

PNG to JPG Converter | Banynova

Password Generator | Banynova

Avoid keyword stuffing or duplicate titles.

---

# 5. Meta Description

Every page should include a unique meta description.

Recommended length:

140–160 characters

Requirements:

• Describe the page clearly

• Encourage clicks

• Reflect actual content

Avoid duplicate descriptions.

---

# 6. Canonical URLs

Every indexable page must define a canonical URL.

Canonical URLs help prevent duplicate indexing.

Verify canonical implementation for:

• Homepage

• Tool Pages

• Blog Posts

• Static Pages

• Category Pages

• Search Pages (when appropriate)

Canonical URLs should always resolve to the preferred version of the page.

---

# 7. Robots Directives

Use robots directives carefully.

Indexable pages:

index, follow

Non-indexable pages may include:

noindex

nofollow

or other directives when justified.

Never block valuable content unintentionally.

---

# 8. Open Graph

Every public page should include Open Graph metadata.

Minimum properties:

og:title

og:description

og:type

og:url

og:image

og:site_name

og:locale

Images should be high quality and suitable for social sharing.

---

# 9. Twitter Cards

Every shareable page should include Twitter metadata.

Recommended:

twitter:card = summary_large_image

Include:

twitter:title

twitter:description

twitter:image

twitter:site (optional)

Maintain consistency with Open Graph metadata.

---

# 10. Social Images

Social preview images should:

• Be unique where practical

• Match page content

• Use high resolution

• Include branding consistently

Recommended size:

1200 × 630 pixels

Avoid text-heavy designs.

---

# 11. Structured Data Philosophy

Structured Data should describe page content for search engines.

Prefer JSON-LD.

Avoid Microdata unless necessary.

Validate all structured data before deployment.

---

# 12. Homepage Structured Data

Homepage should include:

• Organization

• WebSite

• SearchAction

• WebPage

These schemas establish the site's identity and search capabilities.

---

# 13. Tool Page Structured Data

Tool pages should include:

• SoftwareApplication

• BreadcrumbList

• FAQPage (when applicable)

Optional future schemas:

• HowTo

• VideoObject

Use only schemas that accurately represent the page.

---

# 14. Blog Structured Data

Blog posts should include:

• Article

Optional:

• BreadcrumbList

• FAQPage

• Author

• ImageObject

Ensure publication dates are accurate.

---

# 15. Category Page Structured Data

Category pages should include:

• CollectionPage

Optional:

• BreadcrumbList

This improves understanding of grouped content.

---

# 16. Breadcrumbs

Breadcrumb navigation should be implemented for:

• Tool Pages

• Blog Posts

• Documentation

• Categories

Provide both:

• Visible navigation

• BreadcrumbList structured data

---

# 17. Sitemap

Maintain an XML sitemap.

Include:

• Homepage

• Tool Pages

• Blog Posts

• Categories

• Static Pages

Exclude:

• Duplicate URLs

• Error pages

• Non-indexable pages

Update the sitemap automatically whenever possible.

---

# 18. Robots.txt

Maintain a clean robots.txt file.

Allow crawling of public content.

Block:

• Internal search parameters (if necessary)

• Duplicate resources

• Administrative paths

Robots.txt should support—not replace—proper indexing strategies.

---

# 19. URL Standards

URLs should be:

• Short

• Descriptive

• Human-readable

• Lowercase

Use hyphens instead of underscores.

Avoid unnecessary parameters.

Example:

/tools/image-compressor

Avoid:

/page?id=18392

---

# 20. Internal Linking

Create meaningful internal links between related pages.

Examples:

Homepage → Categories

Category → Tools

Blog → Tools

Documentation → Tutorials

Internal linking improves navigation and crawlability.

---

# 21. Heading Structure

Maintain a semantic heading hierarchy.

Requirements:

• One H1

• Logical H2–H6 order

• Descriptive headings

Headings should improve both accessibility and SEO.

---

# 22. Images

Every meaningful image should include:

• Descriptive alt text

• Appropriate filename

• Responsive dimensions

• Optimized format

Image SEO contributes to overall discoverability.

---

# 23. Performance and SEO

Performance directly affects search visibility.

Optimize:

• Core Web Vitals

• LCP

• CLS

• INP

• Mobile performance

Fast pages improve both user experience and search rankings.

---

# 24. Content Standards

Content should be:

• Original

• Helpful

• Accurate

• Well-structured

• Regularly updated

Avoid duplicate or low-value content.

---

# 25. Pagination

When pagination is introduced:

• Preserve logical navigation

• Maintain crawlability

• Avoid duplicate content

Use consistent URL structures.

---

# 26. Validation

Validate:

✓ Meta Tags

✓ Open Graph

✓ Twitter Cards

✓ Structured Data

✓ Canonical URLs

✓ Sitemap

✓ Robots.txt

✓ Rich Results

Perform validation before production deployment.

---

# 27. SEO Checklist

Before publishing:

✓ Unique Title

✓ Unique Description

✓ Canonical URL

✓ Robots Directive

✓ Open Graph

✓ Twitter Cards

✓ JSON-LD

✓ Optimized Images

✓ Internal Links

✓ Structured Heading Hierarchy

---

# 28. Future SEO

Future improvements may include:

• Multilingual SEO

• hreflang

• Video SEO

• Image SEO

• AI-friendly structured content

• Entity optimization

The SEO architecture should remain flexible enough to support future search technologies.

---

# 29. Monitoring

Regularly monitor:

• Search Console

• Index Coverage

• Crawl Errors

• Structured Data Validation

• Core Web Vitals

• Organic Traffic

SEO should be treated as an ongoing engineering process rather than a one-time configuration.

---

# 30. Overall SEO Principle

SEO is the result of clean architecture, semantic markup, accessible design, fast performance, and high-quality content.

Every Banynova page should be engineered to help both users and search engines understand its purpose with minimal ambiguity.

The SEO foundation should remain scalable, standards-compliant, and adaptable to future search engine evolution while preserving long-term maintainability.

# End of Part 9
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 10 — Tool Page Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the engineering standards for all Banynova Tool Pages.

Every tool page should be:

• Fast

• Accessible

• SEO-friendly

• User-focused

• Scalable

• Consistent

Tool pages are the core product of the Banynova platform and should provide a predictable, high-quality user experience.

---

# 2. Tool Page Philosophy

Every tool page should solve one specific problem.

Avoid combining multiple unrelated tools on the same page.

Each tool page should:

• Explain its purpose

• Provide immediate interaction

• Deliver results quickly

• Guide users toward related tools

The primary action should remain visible without unnecessary scrolling.

---

# 3. URL Structure

URLs should be:

• Short

• Human-readable

• Descriptive

• Lowercase

Use hyphens instead of underscores.

Recommended structure:

/tools/image-compressor

/tools/password-generator

/tools/json-formatter

Avoid:

/tool?id=123

/tools/imagecompressor2

URLs should remain stable to preserve SEO value.

---

# 4. Page Layout

Recommended structure:

1. Breadcrumb

2. Hero Section

3. Tool Interface

4. Tool Description

5. Features

6. Usage Instructions

7. FAQ

8. Related Tools

9. Call to Action

10. Footer

Maintain a consistent layout across all tool pages.

---

# 5. Hero Section

The Hero should include:

• Tool Name (H1)

• Short description

• Primary action button

• Optional illustration

Keep introductory content concise.

Users should immediately understand what the tool does.

---

# 6. Tool Interface

The tool interface is the primary content.

Requirements:

• Visible above the fold whenever practical

• Responsive

• Keyboard accessible

• Mobile-friendly

• Fast to load

Avoid unnecessary decorative elements around the tool.

---

# 7. Tool Controls

Controls should follow a predictable order.

Typical flow:

Input

↓

Options

↓

Primary Action

↓

Result

↓

Secondary Actions

Buttons should use clear action verbs.

Examples:

Convert

Generate

Compress

Format

Analyze

---

# 8. Results Section

Results should appear without causing layout shifts.

Include:

• Copy Button

• Download Button (when applicable)

• Reset Button

• Share Button (optional)

Results should remain selectable and accessible.

---

# 9. Related Tools

Every tool page should recommend relevant tools.

Recommended:

4–8 related tools

Selection should prioritize:

• Same category

• Similar workflow

• Frequently used together

Display related tools as reusable tool cards.

---

# 10. FAQ Section

Each tool page should include an FAQ section when appropriate.

Recommended:

3–8 frequently asked questions.

FAQs should:

• Answer real user questions

• Improve usability

• Support SEO

Include FAQPage structured data when FAQs are present.

---

# 11. Breadcrumb Navigation

Provide visible breadcrumb navigation.

Example:

Home

>

Tools

>

Image Tools

>

Image Compressor

Also include:

BreadcrumbList

JSON-LD structured data.

Breadcrumbs improve navigation and search engine understanding.

---

# 12. SoftwareApplication Schema

Every tool page should include:

SoftwareApplication

JSON-LD.

Recommended properties:

• Name

• Description

• Application Category

• Operating System

• URL

• Offers (if applicable)

• Publisher

Use accurate metadata only.

Do not include unsupported properties.

---

# 13. Search Integration

Every tool page should integrate with the global search system.

Search should allow users to discover:

• Other tools

• Categories

• Documentation

• Tutorials

Search behavior should remain consistent across the platform.

---

# 14. Tool Description

Provide a concise explanation below the tool.

Recommended sections:

• Overview

• Key Features

• Benefits

• Supported Formats

Avoid long introductory paragraphs before the tool interface.

---

# 15. Usage Instructions

Explain how to use the tool.

Recommended format:

Step 1

Step 2

Step 3

Step 4

Instructions should be easy to scan.

Avoid unnecessary technical terminology.

---

# 16. Features Section

Highlight the tool's capabilities.

Examples:

• Fast Processing

• Secure

• Browser-Based

• No Installation

• Free to Use

• Mobile Friendly

Keep feature descriptions concise.

---

# 17. Error Handling

Provide clear feedback.

Examples:

Invalid Input

Unsupported File

Empty Field

Network Error

Error messages should explain:

• What happened

• Why

• How to fix it

Avoid generic messages like:

Something went wrong.

---

# 18. Empty States

When no result exists, explain the next step.

Examples:

Upload a file to begin.

Enter text to format.

Generate your first password.

Empty states should guide users toward successful interaction.

---

# 19. Accessibility

Every tool page should support:

✓ Keyboard navigation

✓ Screen readers

✓ Visible focus indicators

✓ Semantic HTML

✓ Reduced motion

✓ WCAG 2.2 AA compliance

Accessibility is required for all interactive controls.

---

# 20. Performance

Tool pages should prioritize:

• Fast initial load

• Optimized images

• Lazy loading for non-critical assets

• Minimal JavaScript

• Efficient rendering

Interactive functionality should remain responsive.

---

# 21. SEO Requirements

Every tool page should include:

✓ Unique Title

✓ Meta Description

✓ Canonical URL

✓ Open Graph

✓ Twitter Cards

✓ SoftwareApplication Schema

✓ Breadcrumb Schema

✓ FAQ Schema (when applicable)

✓ Descriptive H1

✓ Internal links to related tools

---

# 22. Internal Linking

Link to:

• Related tools

• Parent category

• Relevant blog articles

• Tutorials

• Documentation

Internal linking should improve navigation and topical authority.

---

# 23. Responsive Design

Tool pages should adapt across all screen sizes.

Requirements:

• Responsive layouts

• Flexible tool interface

• Readable typography

• Touch-friendly controls

Avoid horizontal scrolling.

---

# 24. Analytics Readiness

Future analytics integration should support:

• Tool usage

• Conversion events

• Search queries

• Copy actions

• Download actions

Analytics should remain modular and privacy-conscious.

---

# 25. Future Scalability

Tool pages should support future enhancements without structural redesign.

Examples:

• AI-assisted features

• User preferences

• Saved history

• Tool ratings

• Comments

• Localization

Core layouts should remain backward-compatible.

---

# 26. Code Review Checklist

Before publishing a tool page:

✓ URL follows standards.

✓ Layout is consistent.

✓ Tool loads correctly.

✓ Related tools included.

✓ FAQ reviewed.

✓ Breadcrumb implemented.

✓ SoftwareApplication schema validated.

✓ Search integration verified.

✓ Accessibility tested.

✓ Performance reviewed.

---

# 27. Quality Standards

Every tool page should provide:

• Clear purpose

• Immediate usability

• Reliable performance

• Consistent design

• Helpful guidance

• Discoverable related content

Users should accomplish their task with minimal effort.

---

# 28. Future Enhancements

Potential future additions include:

• AI-powered recommendations

• Recently used tools

• Personalized suggestions

• Tool collections

• Offline support (PWA)

• Community ratings

These enhancements should integrate without modifying the core tool page architecture.

---

# 29. Maintenance Guidelines

Review tool pages periodically to ensure:

✓ Metadata remains accurate.

✓ Internal links are valid.

✓ Related tools stay relevant.

✓ Structured data validates successfully.

✓ Content reflects current functionality.

Tool pages should evolve without compromising consistency.

---

# 30. Overall Tool Page Principle

A Banynova Tool Page should prioritize usability above all else.
Users should be able to understand the tool, complete their task, and discover related resources with minimal friction.
Every tool page should follow a consistent architecture that supports accessibility, performance, SEO, scalability, and long-term maintainability, ensuring the platform can grow without requiring fundamental structural changes.
# End of Part 10
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 11 — Homepage Standards
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the engineering and design standards for the Banynova Homepage.

The Homepage is the primary entry point to the platform and should clearly communicate the product, guide users to relevant tools, and support long-term scalability.

Every homepage should be:

• Fast

• Accessible

• SEO-friendly

• Responsive

• Discoverable

• Conversion-focused

---

# 2. Homepage Philosophy

The homepage should answer three questions within the first few seconds:

• What is Banynova?

• What can users do here?

• Where should users go next?

The homepage should prioritize clarity over decoration and functionality over complexity.

---

# 3. Homepage Objectives

Primary objectives:

• Introduce Banynova

• Encourage tool discovery

• Support search

• Highlight popular categories

• Increase user engagement

• Improve SEO

• Drive internal navigation

Every section should support one or more of these objectives.

---

# 4. Recommended Homepage Layout

Recommended page order:

1. Header

2. Hero

3. Search

4. Featured Categories

5. Popular Tools

6. Featured Tutorials

7. Platform Statistics

8. Why Choose Banynova

9. Call-to-Action (CTA)

10. Footer

Keep the layout consistent and easy to scan.

---

# 5. Header

The homepage header should include:

• Logo

• Primary Navigation

• Search Shortcut (optional)

• Theme Toggle

• Mobile Menu

Header behavior:

✓ Responsive

✓ Keyboard accessible

✓ Sticky (optional)

✓ Lightweight

Avoid overcrowding the header with secondary links.

---

# 6. Hero Section

The Hero should immediately communicate Banynova's value.

Recommended content:

• H1 headline

• Short description

• Primary CTA

• Secondary CTA

• Hero illustration or mockup

The Hero should remain visually clean and concise.

---

# 7. Search

Homepage search is a primary navigation feature.

Requirements:

✓ Prominently placed

✓ Keyboard accessible

✓ Instant suggestions

✓ Debounced input

✓ Mobile-friendly

Search should help users quickly find tools, categories, tutorials, and documentation.

---

# 8. Featured Categories

Display major tool categories.

Recommended:

6–12 categories

Each category card should include:

• Icon

• Category name

• Short description

• Tool count (optional)

Categories should be visually consistent and reusable.

---

# 9. Popular Tools

Highlight frequently used tools.

Recommended:

8–12 tool cards

Each card should display:

• Icon

• Tool name

• Short description

• Category

• Optional badges (New, Popular, Updated)

Use reusable card components.
---
# 10. Featured Tutorials
Show educational content that helps users get more value from the platform.
Recommended:
3–6 tutorials
Each tutorial card should include:
• Thumbnail (optional)
• Title
• Short description
• Reading time (optional)
• Category
Encourage users to explore beyond individual tools.

---

# 11. Platform Statistics

Display concise, meaningful metrics.

Examples:

• Total Tools

• Categories

• Tutorials

• Monthly Users (if applicable)

• Supported Formats

Use simple, readable cards.

Avoid exaggerated or misleading numbers.

---

# 12. Why Choose Banynova

Highlight platform advantages.

Examples:

• Free to Use

• Browser-Based

• Privacy-Focused

• Fast Performance

• No Installation

• Mobile Friendly

Present these as concise feature cards with icons.

---

# 13. Call-to-Action (CTA)

Include a clear CTA near the end of the homepage.

Examples:

• Explore All Tools

• Browse Categories

• Start Converting

• Read Tutorials

CTA buttons should use action-oriented labels.

Avoid multiple competing primary CTAs.

---

# 14. Cards

All homepage cards should follow the shared component system.

Requirements:

✓ Equal spacing

✓ Consistent radius

✓ Reusable design

✓ Responsive layout

✓ Accessible interactions

Cards should support Light and Dark themes without separate implementations.

---

# 15. Search Integration

Homepage search should integrate with the global search index.

Users should be able to discover:

• Tools

• Categories

• Tutorials

• Documentation

Results should remain consistent with search behavior across the rest of the platform.

---

# 16. Motion System

Motion should reinforce hierarchy and interaction without becoming distracting.

Recommended motion:

• Hero illustration floating

• Button hover feedback

• Card elevation on hover

• Navigation underline animation

• Search result reveal

• Section fade-in (optional)

Animations should:

✓ Be GPU-friendly

✓ Use transform and opacity

✓ Avoid layout-triggering properties

✓ Never delay interaction

---

# 17. Motion Accessibility

Respect user preferences.

Implement:

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)

Reduce or disable:

• Floating animations

• Continuous loops

• Hover motion

• Parallax

• Decorative transitions

All essential functionality must remain available without animation.

---

# 18. Responsive Design

Homepage should adapt smoothly across all screen sizes.

Requirements:

• Mobile-first layout

• Flexible grids

• Readable typography

• Touch-friendly controls

• No horizontal scrolling

Breakpoints should be based on layout needs rather than specific devices.

---

# 19. Accessibility

Homepage must support:

✓ WCAG 2.2 AA

✓ Keyboard navigation

✓ Screen readers

✓ Semantic HTML

✓ Visible focus indicators

✓ Sufficient color contrast

✓ Skip-to-content link

Accessibility should be validated before release.

---

# 20. Performance

Homepage should prioritize fast loading.

Recommendations:

• Optimize LCP elements

• Lazy-load below-the-fold images

• Inline Critical CSS

• Minimize JavaScript

• Optimize fonts

• Compress images

• Reduce unnecessary network requests

Performance should be monitored continuously.

---

# 21. SEO

Homepage should include:

✓ Unique Title

✓ Meta Description

✓ Canonical URL

✓ Open Graph

✓ Twitter Cards

✓ JSON-LD:

- Organization
- WebSite
- SearchAction
- WebPage

✓ XML Sitemap inclusion

✓ Robots directives

Ensure metadata accurately reflects the homepage.

---

# 22. Future Scalability

Homepage architecture should support future modules without structural redesign.

Potential future additions:

• Recently Added Tools

• Trending Tools

• AI Recommendations

• User Collections

• Testimonials

• Newsletter

• Featured Integrations

Core layout components should remain backward-compatible so future modules can be integrated without breaking existing templates.

---

# 23. Code Review Checklist

Before publishing the homepage:

✓ Layout follows the standard structure.

✓ Hero communicates the platform clearly.

✓ Search works correctly.

✓ Categories are organized logically.

✓ Tool cards are reusable.

✓ Tutorials are relevant.

✓ Statistics are accurate.

✓ CTA is prominent.

✓ Footer includes essential links.

✓ Motion respects reduced-motion preferences.

✓ Accessibility has been tested.

✓ Performance meets Core Web Vitals targets.

✓ SEO metadata and structured data validate successfully.

---

# 24. Quality Standards

A production-ready homepage should:

• Load quickly

• Guide users intuitively

• Support exploration

• Encourage engagement

• Scale gracefully

• Maintain visual consistency

Every section should provide value and justify its presence.

---
# 25. Overall Homepage Principle
The Banynova Homepage should function as the central gateway to the platform.
It should help users discover tools, understand the platform, and navigate efficiently while maintaining excellent accessibility, performance, SEO, and usability.
The homepage architecture should follow a component-first design system that supports incremental growth, enabling new sections and features to be added without requiring fundamental redesign or compromising the consistency of the overall user experience.
# End of Part 11
# ==========================================================================
# ==========================================================================
# BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
# Part 12 — Production Checklist
# Version: 1.0
# ==========================================================================

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the final production readiness checklist for Banynova.

Every page, component, and feature should pass this checklist before deployment.

The objective is to ensure:

• High quality

• Performance

• Accessibility

• Maintainability

• Security

• Scalability

• Consistency

This checklist serves as the final engineering verification prior to release.

---

# 2. Production Philosophy

Deployment is not the end of development.

Every release should be:

• Stable

• Predictable

• Well-tested

• Backward-compatible

• Fully documented

Never deploy features that bypass the engineering standards.

---

# 3. Code Review Checklist

Before merging code, verify:

✓ Naming conventions are consistent.

✓ Components are reusable.

✓ No duplicated logic exists.

✓ No unnecessary complexity.

✓ HTML is semantic.

✓ CSS follows the design token system.

✓ JavaScript modules have a single responsibility.

✓ Comments explain architecture—not obvious code.

✓ Dead code has been removed.

✓ Code formatting is consistent.

✓ No console.log() statements remain in production code.

✓ No commented-out legacy code remains.

---

# 4. HTML Review

Verify:

✓ Valid HTML5.

✓ One H1 per page.

✓ Proper heading hierarchy.

✓ Semantic elements used.

✓ Labels associated with all form controls.

✓ Meaningful alt text.

✓ Accessible landmarks.

✓ Valid metadata.

✓ Clean document structure.

---

# 5. CSS Review

Verify:

✓ Design Tokens are used consistently.

✓ No duplicate styles.

✓ No unnecessary specificity.

✓ Mobile-first responsive design.

✓ Components remain reusable.

✓ Utility classes remain minimal.

✓ No hardcoded colors.

✓ Motion follows standards.

✓ Reduced-motion support implemented.

---

# 6. JavaScript Review

Verify:

✓ Modular architecture.

✓ No global variables.

✓ Event delegation where appropriate.

✓ Debounce applied to input events.

✓ Throttle applied to scroll events.

✓ Theme initialization prevents FOUC.

✓ Search is optimized.

✓ No unused modules.

✓ No blocking synchronous tasks.

---

# 7. Performance Audit

Verify:

✓ Lighthouse Performance ≥ 95.

✓ LCP ≤ 2.5 seconds.

✓ CLS ≤ 0.1.

✓ INP ≤ 200 milliseconds.

✓ Minimal JavaScript execution.

✓ Efficient rendering.

✓ Images optimized.

✓ Fonts optimized.

✓ Critical CSS applied.

✓ Lazy loading implemented correctly.

✓ Network requests minimized.

✓ No unnecessary third-party resources.

---

# 8. Accessibility Audit

Verify WCAG 2.2 AA compliance.

Checklist:

✓ Keyboard navigation.

✓ Screen reader compatibility.

✓ Visible focus indicators.

✓ Logical tab order.

✓ Proper ARIA usage.

✓ Semantic HTML.

✓ Skip-to-content link.

✓ Color contrast meets AA.

✓ Reduced-motion support.

✓ Accessible dialogs.

✓ Accessible forms.

✓ Accessible search.

Accessibility testing should be performed using both automated tools and manual review.

---

# 9. SEO Audit

Verify:

✓ Unique title.

✓ Unique description.

✓ Canonical URL.

✓ Open Graph metadata.

✓ Twitter Cards.

✓ Robots directives.

✓ JSON-LD validation.

✓ Structured data passes Rich Results Test.

✓ Sitemap updated.

✓ Robots.txt verified.

✓ Internal links reviewed.

✓ No broken links.

---

# 10. Browser Testing

Test the latest stable versions of:

✓ Google Chrome

✓ Mozilla Firefox

✓ Microsoft Edge

✓ Apple Safari

Verify:

• Layout

• Navigation

• Forms

• Search

• Dialogs

• Animations

• Theme switching

Avoid browser-specific implementations whenever possible.

---

# 11. Mobile Testing

Test on multiple viewport sizes.

Verify:

✓ Responsive layout.

✓ Touch-friendly controls.

✓ Navigation.

✓ Search.

✓ Forms.

✓ Dialogs.

✓ Images.

✓ Typography.

✓ No horizontal scrolling.

✓ Comfortable touch targets.

Mobile performance should receive the same priority as desktop.

---

# 12. Theme Verification

Verify:

✓ Light Theme.

✓ Dark Theme.

✓ Theme persistence.

✓ No flash of incorrect theme.

✓ Accessible contrast in both themes.

✓ Components render consistently.

---

# 13. Motion Verification

Verify:

✓ GPU-friendly animations.

✓ No layout-triggering animations.

✓ Hover effects remain subtle.

✓ Motion never delays interaction.

✓ prefers-reduced-motion is respected.

✓ Continuous animations can be disabled.

Animations should enhance usability rather than distract from it.

---

# 14. Security Review

Verify:

✓ User input is sanitized.

✓ No unsafe HTML injection.

✓ No use of eval().

✓ Sensitive information is never stored in localStorage.

✓ External links use appropriate security attributes when opening new tabs.

✓ Dependencies are reviewed.

Platform limitations (such as Blogger-managed security headers) should be documented where applicable.

---

# 15. Content Review

Verify:

✓ Grammar reviewed.

✓ Spelling reviewed.

✓ Consistent terminology.

✓ Helpful instructions.

✓ Accurate documentation.

✓ Updated screenshots (when applicable).

✓ No placeholder text.

✓ No Lorem Ipsum.

---

# 16. Future Scalability Review

Verify:

✓ Components remain reusable.

✓ Modules remain independent.

✓ Architecture supports future growth.

✓ New pages follow existing standards.

✓ No page-specific hacks.

✓ Core layouts remain backward-compatible.

✓ Future integrations will not require structural redesign.

The component-first architecture should support incremental platform growth.

---

# 17. Deployment Checklist

Before deployment:

✓ Final code review completed.

✓ Performance audit passed.

✓ Accessibility audit passed.

✓ SEO audit passed.

✓ Browser testing completed.

✓ Mobile testing completed.

✓ Structured data validated.

✓ Sitemap regenerated or verified.

✓ Robots.txt reviewed.

✓ Version number updated (if applicable).

✓ Backup created.

✓ Deployment approved.

✓ Production environment verified.

---

# 18. Post-Deployment Verification

Immediately after deployment:

✓ Homepage loads correctly.

✓ Navigation works.

✓ Search functions correctly.

✓ Tool pages load.

✓ Theme switching works.

✓ Images load properly.

✓ No JavaScript errors.

✓ No broken internal links.

✓ Structured data remains valid.

✓ Analytics (if enabled) is receiving data.

---

# 19. Continuous Improvement

After each release:

• Review performance metrics.

• Monitor accessibility issues.

• Analyze user feedback.

• Fix regressions promptly.

• Update engineering documentation.

• Refactor when justified.

Engineering standards should evolve alongside the platform without sacrificing backward compatibility.

---

# 20. Release Approval Criteria

A release is considered production-ready only when:

✓ Engineering Standards are satisfied.

✓ Quality Assurance is completed.

✓ Accessibility meets WCAG 2.2 AA.

✓ Performance targets are achieved.

✓ SEO requirements are validated.

✓ No critical or high-severity issues remain.

If any critical requirement fails, deployment should be postponed until the issue is resolved.

---

# 21. Overall Production Principle
Production readiness is achieved through disciplined engineering—not last-minute fixes.
Every Banynova release should demonstrate consistency, reliability, accessibility, performance, security, and maintainability.
The engineering foundation should remain component-first, standards-driven, and backward-compatible, ensuring that future enhancements can be integrated without compromising the stability, quality, or long-term scalability of the platform.
# End of Part 12
